usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
pleach — parallel, isolated work sessions for multi-repo workspaces.

Usage: pleach <command> [arguments]

Sessions
  new <name> [sub …]        Create a session: one git worktree per repo on branch session/<name>.
  open <name> [command …]   Open the session (creating it if needed). Default command: claude.
  ls [-l|--json]            List sessions. With -l, detail each one's repos; --json to script it.
  cd [<session>]            Change into the session (needs: eval "$(pleach shell-init)").
  path [<session>]          Print where a session lives (--canonical for the canonical).
  add [<session>] <sub …>   Mount sub-repos into an existing session.
  sync [<session>|--all]    Bring the canonical's local main into the session's branches.
  rm <name> [--force] [--reap]  Remove the session. Refuses when work is pending.
  prune [--apply]           List (and with --apply remove) fully integrated sessions.
  clean [<session>]         Delete git-ignored artifacts (node_modules, builds…).
  each [--repos] '<cmd>'    Run a command in the canonical and in every session.
  conflicts                 Report what parallel sessions would break in each other at merge.

Workspace
  init [--default]          Adopt pleach in the current repo (writes .pleach.conf).
  repos [--sync]            Compare repos on disk x conf x sessions; --sync aligns everything.
  doctor [--fix]            Check the workspace for drift, stale locks and port clashes.

Tool
  skill [--install]         Emit (or install) the agent skill that teaches an LLM to drive pleach.
  shell-init                Emit the shell wrapper that enables `pleach cd`.
  completions <bash|zsh>    Emit the completion script for your shell.
  install                   Copy this script to ~/.local/bin/pleach.
  update                    Self-update from the repo's main branch.
  version                   Print the installed version.
  help [command]            Detailed help for a command (and `help config`).

e.g. pleach open fix-login          create/open and get to work
     pleach repos --sync            adopt a new workspace repo (e.g. payments)
     pleach sync fix-login          bring the session up to date with main
     pleach skill --install         teach your LLM to drive pleach
EOF
}

help_topic() {
  case "$1" in
    new) cat <<'EOF'
pleach new <name> [sub …] [--from <session|ref>] [--fetch] [--no-bootstrap]

Creates the session at <PLEACH_DIR>/<name>: one git worktree per repo (root +
subs), all on branch session/<name>, cut from the base (default: main). Copies
untracked secrets (.env*, .dev.vars*, PLEACH_SECRETS_EXTRA), assigns an exclusive
block of 100 ports (.session-env) and runs the dependency bootstrap.

  sub ...          Only these sub-repos (default: everything in PLEACH_SUBS).
  --from X         Cut from X instead of the base. X is either the name of another
                   session (resolving to its branch, in every repo) or any git
                   ref. This is how you stack work: a session that builds on
                   another one's UNMERGED commits rather than waiting for them.
                   A repo where X does not exist falls back to the base and says
                   so -- one repo never blocks the others.
  --fetch          Update the base from origin before creating.
  --no-bootstrap   Skip dependency installation.

If branch session/<name> already exists in a repo, it is resumed, not recreated.

What a stacked session costs: it integrates against a moving target. `sync` still
brings the base in, not the parent session -- and when the parent is rebased or
squashed at merge, the child carries commits that no longer exist upstream.
`ls -l` marks these sessions with what they were cut from, for that reason.
EOF
    ;;
    open) cat <<'EOF'
pleach open <name> [command ...]

Enters the session (creating it with every sub-repo if it does not exist), loads
.session-env (ports, identity) and runs the command in there. Default: claude.

  pleach open fix-x             claude inside the session
  pleach open fix-x claude -r   resume that session's previous conversation
  pleach open fix-x codex       any other LLM/command
  pleach open fix-x code        VS Code on the session's multi-root workspace

`open` does that sourcing, and so does `pleach cd` once shell-init is installed.
A shell you opened yourself has NOT: cd in by hand, run your dev server, and it
binds whatever default the app has — port 3000 on top of a colleague. Run
`source .session-env` first.

The block gives the root PORT and every sub-repo its own PLEACH_PORT_<SUB>, so a
workspace with three services can run all three at once. They read $PORT, so point
each one at its own variable (PORT=$PLEACH_PORT_API npm run dev, or a two-line
dev script) — pleach allocates the ports, it does not start your servers.
EOF
    ;;
    ls) cat <<'EOF'
pleach ls [-l]

With no flags, lists just the sessions — name, index, port block and repo count.
It is instant (runs no git).

  -l, --long   Per-repo detail: branch, commits ahead of base (+N), uncommitted
               changes and commits behind the local base. Per session: last
               activity (date of the most recent commit) and the "fully
               integrated - removable" mark when nothing is ahead and nothing is
               uncommitted. Slower (several git calls per repo).
  --json       The same map, machine-readable: name, index, port block, runtime
               slug, path, branch and mounted sub-repos. This is pleach's agent
               surface — a shell-out works in any tool that can run a command,
               which is why there is no MCP server (see the README).
EOF
    ;;
    add) cat <<'EOF'
pleach add [<session>] <sub ...|--all> [--no-bootstrap]

Mounts sub-repos into an existing session — the typical case is a new repo in the
workspace (e.g. payments) that older sessions do not have. Creates the worktree on
branch session/<name>, copies THAT sub's secrets and runs ITS bootstrap.

  --all            Mount every workspace sub-repo the session is missing.
  --no-bootstrap   Skip dependency installation.

The session name is optional when the command runs inside it:

  pleach add fix-x payments       from outside
  pleach add payments             from inside session fix-x
  pleach repos --sync             to do it across ALL sessions at once
EOF
    ;;
    sync) cat <<'EOF'
pleach sync [<session>|--all] [--rebase] [--fetch] [--any-branch] [--dry-run] [--yes]

Brings a session up to date with the canonical: in each repo it integrates the
LOCAL <base> (the canonical's main) into branch session/<name>. It never reaches
for origin — the local main is the source. Update it as usual (git pull in the
canonical) or pass --fetch.

  --all          Every session. Asks for confirmation (shows the blast radius);
                 non-interactive callers (scripts/agents) must pass --yes.
  --dry-run, -n  Show what would be integrated/skipped, changing NOTHING (not
                 even the base ref — --fetch is ignored).
  --yes, -y      Skip the --all confirmation (for deliberate automation).
  --fetch        Before integrating, try to advance the local <base> from origin.
  --rebase       Rebase instead of merge (rewrites history — force-push if a PR exists).
  --any-branch   Integrate even into worktrees not on the session's branch.

Safety rules (a skipped repo never blocks the others):
  - A worktree not on session/<name> is SKIPPED. If you switched to feat/x in
    there, sync leaves it alone — unrequested merges into feature branches are
    exactly what this guard rail exists to prevent.
  - A repo with uncommitted changes is SKIPPED (never an automatic stash).
  - A conflict aborts that repo and leaves it untouched; the command tells you
    where to resolve it by hand.
  - Workspace sub-repos missing from the session are reported, not mounted
    (mounting is `pleach add`'s explicit job).

With no name and outside a session it is an error. Inside a session, the name is
inferred.
EOF
    ;;
    rm) cat <<'EOF'
pleach rm <name> [--force] [--reap]

Removes the session's worktrees and its folder. Refuses if any repo has
uncommitted changes or commits on session/<name> not yet integrated into the base.

  --force   Remove anyway. Branches with unintegrated commits are ALWAYS
            preserved (delete by hand: git -C <repo> branch -D session/<name>).
  --reap    Kill processes still listening inside the session's port block. By
            default they are only reported: a dev server outlives the worktree
            that started it, and killing a process is not something a cleanup
            command should do behind your back.

"Integrated" is judged against the LOCAL base — if the merge only happened on the
remote, run git fetch in the repo before rm.
EOF
    ;;
    prune) cat <<'EOF'
pleach prune [--apply]

Post-merge hygiene: finds the sessions whose work is fully integrated into the
base (same criterion as rm — no uncommitted changes, no session/<name> commits
outside the base) and, with --apply, removes them through rm's safe path.

With no flags it is a dry run: it only lists. "Integrated" is judged against the
LOCAL base — merges that only landed on the remote need a git fetch first.
EOF
    ;;
    clean) cat <<'EOF'
pleach clean [<session>] [--apply] [--bootstrap]

Long-lived sessions rematerialize disk (node_modules, .next, dist, ...). clean
finds those directories INSIDE the session and deletes them — but ONLY the ones
git ignores: a tracked dist/ is preserved and reported. Dry run by default, with
sizes.

  --apply       Actually delete.
  --bootstrap   Implies --apply; re-runs the bootstrap afterwards (reinstalls deps).

Inside a session the name is inferred. Targets: node_modules .next .open-next
.wrangler dist build target .venv vendor .turbo
EOF
    ;;
    each) cat <<'EOF'
pleach each [--repos] '<command>'
pleach each [--repos] <cmd> <arg> ...

Runs the command in the CANONICAL and in EVERY session, with a header per target.
A single argument goes through bash -c (pipes and globs work); several arguments
are executed literally. The exit code aggregates (non-zero if any failed).

  --repos   Descend into every mounted sub-repo as well, not just the roots.
            Targets are labelled "<session> · <repo>", the root first.

Without --repos only the roots are entered, and that is the default deliberately:
each runs YOUR command, so where it lands is part of what you asked for. The
commands that always descend — sync, ls -l, doctor — are running operations pleach
itself defines. Use `--` before a command that starts with a dash.

Typical use: cross-session visibility — parallel work in other sessions is
invisible to your worktree until it merges. e.g.:

  pleach each 'ls docs/rfcs'                RFC numbers taken EVERYWHERE
  pleach each 'git log --oneline -1'        last commit in each root
  pleach each --repos 'git status -s'       uncommitted work in every repo
EOF
    ;;
    conflicts) cat <<'EOF'
pleach conflicts

Answers, before you merge anything, what parallel sessions have done to each
other. Three separate questions, reported separately because they are not equally
serious:

  WOULD CONFLICT   git performs the three-way merge between each pair of session
                   branches in memory (merge-tree --write-tree) and reports what
                   it cannot resolve. This is git's own verdict, not a guess. The
                   working tree, the index and the branches are left untouched —
                   only loose objects enter the object store. Needs
                   git >= 2.38; below that the check is skipped and says so.

                   It reports the CHANGE, not just the file: how many conflicting
                   regions, where each begins, and what both sides put in the
                   first one. A filename cannot tell two edits to adjacent lines
                   from a rewrite. The content is read back out of the merged
                   tree merge-tree has just written.

                   Every session is also merged against the BASE on its own, so
                   "can this one land at all" is answered even when every other
                   session has gone. Those read <session> <-> <base>.

                   A session whose work has already LANDED is skipped, so a
                   verdict expires with the conflict it describes. Landing is
                   decidable when it was a true merge: the branch becomes an
                   ancestor of the base. After a SQUASH or a rebase it is not,
                   and the fallback - branch and base holding identical content -
                   stops being true once the base moves on. A squash-landed
                   session can therefore still be reported; remove it with
                   `pleach rm`.

  ALSO TOUCHED     the same file appears in more than one session but git merges
                   it cleanly today -- or the work is not committed yet, so no
                   merge can be decided. Worth knowing, not worth stopping for.

  SAME SLOT        two sessions claiming one numbered slot with different
                   filenames -- 0004_billing.sql against 0004_last_login.sql.
                   git merges these CLEANLY, because they are different files,
                   and lands two migrations numbered 0004. The sequence is a
                   shared namespace living inside the filename, and nothing in
                   git models it. Covers the NNNN_name / NNN-name convention.

ALSO TOUCHED and SAME SLOT cover every repo of every session, counting committed
work since the session branched as well as uncommitted changes. WOULD CONFLICT
reads the branch refs, so it sees committed work only -- an uncommitted
resolution is invisible to it.

Worktrees move collisions from the working tree to merge time, which is the
point. The side effect is that they also HIDE them until then: parallel work in
another session is invisible to yours by construction.

Always exits 0. An overlap is a normal, resolvable state -- the value is knowing
early enough to split the work differently.
EOF
    ;;
    path) cat <<'EOF'
pleach path [<session>] [--canonical]

Prints the absolute path of a session — the primitive everything scriptable is
built on (the shell wrapper, completions, your own aliases).

  --canonical   Print the canonical checkout instead.

Inside a session the name is inferred. An unknown session is an error, so
`cd "$(pleach path x)"` fails loudly instead of landing you in $HOME.
EOF
    ;;
    cd) cat <<'EOF'
pleach cd [<session>]

Changes your shell into the session and sources its .session-env (ports and
runtime identity land in that shell).

This one command cannot work from the binary alone: a child process cannot change
its parent's directory. It needs the wrapper:

  eval "$(pleach shell-init)"      # add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc

Without the wrapper the command explains itself rather than failing silently.
EOF
    ;;
    skill) cat <<'EOF'
pleach skill [--install | --project | --dir <path>]

Emits the agent skill: a single Markdown file, with the frontmatter the skill
runtimes expect, that teaches an LLM to drive pleach — the boundary between the
canonical and a session, the runtime state git does not isolate, which commands
only report and which ones act, and how to read the map as JSON instead of
scraping human output.

With no flags it prints to stdout, which is the portable form: pipe it into any
tool, any model, any file your runtime reads.

  --install     Write to ~/.claude/skills/pleach/SKILL.md — personal, every project.
  --project     Write to ./.claude/skills/pleach/SKILL.md — commit it and the whole
                team's agents get it.
  --dir P       Write to P/pleach/SKILL.md — for any other runtime's skills folder.

The skill lives inside this script, so a `pleach` copied anywhere still emits it.
What is written is a COPY: re-run this after upgrading pleach.

Needs no workspace and no .pleach.conf — install it before adopting pleach.
EOF
      ;;
    shell-init) cat <<'EOF'
pleach shell-init

Emits a shell function that wraps pleach so that `pleach cd` can change your
shell's directory. Everything other than `cd` is passed straight through to the
real command. Add to ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc:

  eval "$(pleach shell-init)"

Works in bash and zsh.
EOF
    ;;
    completions) cat <<'EOF'
pleach completions <bash|zsh>

Emits the completion script for your shell. Completes commands, `help` topics,
and the names of existing sessions for the commands that take one.

  eval "$(pleach completions zsh)"      # add to ~/.zshrc
  eval "$(pleach completions bash)"     # add to ~/.bashrc
EOF
    ;;
    doctor) cat <<'EOF'
pleach doctor [--fix]

Asks out loud everything a multi-repo tool can get wrong quietly:

  - sub-repos declared in PLEACH_SUBS that are no longer on disk;
  - a lock left behind by a run that died mid-way;
  - worktrees git still believes in whose folder is gone (these make the next
    `new` fail with a confusing "already used by worktree");
  - sessions whose creation never finished — killed or failed partway, they carry
    every artefact a finished one has, so nothing else tells them apart;
  - sessions with no .session-env, duplicate indexes or overlapping port blocks;
  - worktrees sitting on a branch other than session/<name>;
  - processes still listening inside a session's port block.

Reports by default and exits non-zero when it finds a problem, so it fits in a
CI step or a shell prompt.

  --fix   Perform only repairs that cannot lose work: release a stale lock, write
          a fresh .session-env for a session that lost one (with a free index, so
          it never inherits another session's port block), and run
          `git worktree repair` before pruning registrations that are still stale
          afterwards. Repair comes first deliberately — a worktree whose folder
          MOVED and one whose folder is GONE look identical from the registration
          side, and pruning the first destroys the link that would have restored
          it. Nothing else is automatic: finishing an interrupted creation may
          install dependencies, so it stays a command you choose to run.
EOF
    ;;
    version) cat <<'EOF'
pleach version

Prints the installed version (aliases: --version, -V). Handy for confirming which
install path (npm/bun global, or ~/.local/bin) is active on your PATH.
EOF
    ;;
    init) cat <<'EOF'
pleach init [--default]

Adopts pleach in the current repo: detects first-level sub-repos and writes
.pleach.conf at the root.

  --default   Also record this project as the machine default in
              ~/.config/pleach/config (PLEACH_DEFAULT_CANONICAL), which makes
              `pleach ls`/`new` work from any directory.
EOF
    ;;
    repos) cat <<'EOF'
pleach repos [--sync] [--no-bootstrap]

Shows the workspace's repos in three views — what is on the canonical's disk, what
.pleach.conf declares in PLEACH_SUBS, and what each session is missing. This is
the command for adopting a new workspace repo.

With no flags it is read-only. With --sync:
  1. appends the new repos to PLEACH_SUBS in .pleach.conf (a surgical edit of the
     line — comments and your pleach_bootstrap hook stay intact; if the conf does
     not declare PLEACH_SUBS, auto-detection already covers them);
  2. mounts the missing worktrees in ALL existing sessions, copying secrets and
     running each new sub's bootstrap.

  --no-bootstrap   Mount without installing dependencies.

Repos declared in the conf but absent from disk are reported, never removed.
EOF
    ;;
    install) cat <<'EOF'
pleach install

Copies this script to ~/.local/bin/pleach (run it from a clone of the repo).
Installs via npm/bun do not need this.
EOF
    ;;
    update) cat <<'EOF'
pleach update

Self-updates the copy at ~/.local/bin/pleach from the repo's main branch over
plain https (curl, no auth needed). Validates the downloaded script's syntax
before installing it. Installs managed by npm/bun are detected and redirected to
the package manager.
EOF
    ;;
    config) cat <<'EOF'
.pleach.conf — a bash file (sourced) at the canonical's root. Everything optional.

  PLEACH_CANONICAL=/path      If this checkout is NOT the canonical (replicas).
  PLEACH_SUBS=(subA subB)     Sub-repos per session. Default: auto-detect
                              first-level dirs with .git. Align with disk:
                              pleach repos --sync
  PLEACH_BASE=main            Base branch for sessions.
  PLEACH_DIR=/path/.sessions  Where sessions live.
  PLEACH_PORT_START=10000     Start of the port blocks (session N gets +N*100).
                              Inside a block, the root gets PORT (= the base) and
                              every mounted sub-repo gets PLEACH_PORT_<SUB> at
                              base+1, base+2, … — because one PORT for a workspace
                              with three services means the second one dies on
                              EADDRINUSE inside a single session. Point each
                              service's dev script at its own variable.
  PLEACH_SECRETS_EXTRA=(...)  Extra relative paths to copy into each session.
  pleach_bootstrap() { ... }  Post-creation hook. $1 = session folder,
                              $2 = sub being mounted (empty = whole session, in
                              `new`). Default: bun install where a package.json
                              is found.

Canonical resolution, in order:
  1. env PLEACH_CANONICAL
  2. .session-env walking up from the cwd (we are inside a session)
  3. .pleach.conf walking up (may redirect via PLEACH_CANONICAL)
  4. ~/.config/pleach/config -> PLEACH_DEFAULT_CANONICAL

  2 before 3 on purpose: both files sit in a session's root worktree, because
  the conf is meant to be committed. .session-env is the more specific answer.

Guard rail for automation/harnesses (env, not conf):
  PLEACH_EXPECT_CANONICAL=/path   If the RESOLVED canonical differs from this
                                  path, pleach aborts before any write. Mandatory
                                  in test scripts — it stops a wrong cwd from
                                  resolving to your real workspace.
EOF
    ;;
    *) err "no help for '$1' (commands: init new open ls path cd add sync rm prune clean each conflicts repos doctor shell-init completions install update version; topics: config)" ;;
  esac
}

cmd_help() {
  if [ $# -eq 0 ]; then usage; else help_topic "$1"; fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ $# -ge 1 ] || { usage; exit 0; }
cmd="$1"; shift
case "$cmd" in
  init)    cmd_init "$@" ;;
  new)     cmd_new "$@" ;;
  open)    cmd_open "$@" ;;
  add)     cmd_add "$@" ;;
  sync)    cmd_sync "$@" ;;
  repos)   cmd_repos "$@" ;;
  rm)      cmd_rm "$@" ;;
  prune)   cmd_prune "$@" ;;
  clean)   cmd_clean "$@" ;;
  each)    cmd_each "$@" ;;
  ls)      cmd_ls "$@" ;;
  path)    cmd_path "$@" ;;
  cd)      cmd_cd "$@" ;;
  doctor)  cmd_doctor "$@" ;;
  conflicts) cmd_conflicts "$@" ;;
  completions) cmd_completions "$@" ;;
  shell-init)  cmd_shell_init "$@" ;;
  skill)   cmd_skill "$@" ;;
  install) cmd_install "$@" ;;
  update)  cmd_update "$@" ;;
  version|--version|-V) echo "pleach $PLEACH_VERSION" ;;
  help|-h|--help) cmd_help "$@" ;;
  *) err "unknown command: $cmd (init|new|open|add|sync|repos|rm|prune|clean|each|ls|path|cd|doctor|conflicts|skill|completions|shell-init|install|update|version|help)" ;;
esac
